      "text": "��<!DOCTYPE html PUBLIC \"-//W3C//DTD XHTML 1.0 Transitional//EN\" \"http://www.w3.org/TR/xhtml1/DTD/xhtml1-transitional.dtd\"><html><body><p><h3><span style=\"line-height: 20px; font-size: 14px;\">Carl Jung, a Swiss psychiatrist, was an associate of�</span><a title=\"Sigmund Freud\" class=\"autolink\" style=\"line-height: 20px; font-size: 14px;\" href=\"http://moodle.eipsnextstep.ca/mod/book/view.php?id=18448\">Sigmund Freud</a><span style=\"line-height: 20px; font-size: 14px;\">. They parted company because they disagreed about important psychological theories and ideas.</span></h3>\n<div class=\"no-overflow\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><img width=\"234\" height=\"195\" src=\"http://www.dreamlab.com.au/wp-content/uploads/2011/12/2136573.jpg\"></p>\n<p>Like Freud, Jung spent a great deal of time studying the unconscious mind. However these great psychologists had <strong>differing views on the factors that determine human behavior.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Freud</strong> believed that behavior is determined by our early <strong>childhood experiences</strong> while <strong>Jung</strong> emphasized that <strong>goals play an important role in directing human behavior</strong>.</p>\n<p><img width=\"727\" height=\"545\" style=\"margin-right: auto; margin-left: auto; display: block;\" src=\"http://image.slidesharecdn.com/psychodynamictheoriesofpersonality-110225191047-phpapp02/95/psychodynamic-theories-of-personality-19-728.jpg?cb=1298661078\"></p>\n</div></p></body></html>",
